+# Chapter 6: implementation and boundary notes
+
+## Owners and completion
+
+The existing Engine still renders and sends one request. `Parse(*http.Response, StreamCallbacks)` is the only production parser seam; plain JSON and accumulated SSE both use the inherited canonical decoder. The parser owns response-local IDs, allocated from zero when parts first appear. It forwards owned frame bytes with the configured key removed. Raw callbacks may contain provider-private fields and must not be published. Root `SetAPITrace`/`TraceFrame` serializes a caller-owned diagnostic sink; the built-in trace additionally removes signature/reasoning/authorization-shaped fields. The entry points open `api.log` privately. Wire traces are not durable conversation history.
+
+SSE framing handles CRLF, comments, blank separators, multiline data, pending EOF dispatch, trailers and reader errors. Provider accumulators retain signatures and opaque replay data without turning them into visible chunks. Tool JSON is not decoded until a complete response. Final canonical decoding and usage retain inherited rules (including unknown-usage failure, distinct from measured zero).
+
+The worker forwards deltas into the existing actor inbox, tagged with turn and response identity. The actor rejects stale reports; only it changes history, usage-facing state, tool decisions, and replies. A complete response plus its ID list travels in the reliable worker report, independently of best-effort fan-out. The actor commits and saves the response before final notifications. IDs are never persisted as dialogue and may repeat in the next response. Structural tool-result finals retain their separate non-response scope.
+
+A request-scoped `AskWatching` callback is a thin adapter to the same actor and reliable request result; it must promptly queue work. Existing `Observe` has the same nonblocking-consumer obligation. Observations are advisory; neither their arrival nor their absence completes a request.
+
+Tool authorization requires both visible nonblank text arriving before the first tool delta and visible finalized text before the first tool call. Reasoning and text arriving after the first call do not authorize side effects. The existing all-batch rejection, recovery, policy-off behavior, batch budget, jobs, cancellation and admission rules remain in place.
+
+## Application lifetime
+
+The CLI observer copies into a mutex-protected display queue and does not perform terminal I/O on the actor. The display goroutine is the sole stdout owner. Provider/hint origin is explicit; literal model text `hint:early` stays on stdout. Reply text is emitted once from deltas; finals carry identity without printing it again. Human stderr color is terminal-only. The inherited chat marker and protocol reliable-result records remain compatible. Shutdown closes admission, joins the root workers and reliable request waiters, closes the queue, drains it, then closes trace/history resources. Thus no late producer writes to a closed display queue during normal shutdown.
+
+`EN_DISABLE_STREAMING` is the core toggle. Separate module `examples/ch07` maps `CH07_NO_STREAM` to Config and measures actual counts/first-delta latency through the public facade. The existing `examples/ch06` collaboration workflow remains separate. Catalogued stream masks are independent of media masks; unknown model IDs stay conservative. Configured model aliases remain request selectors while resolved response models stamp replay provenance.
+
+## Test migration and evidence
+
+The Parse and Render signatures intentionally changed in this chapter. Existing test call sites are mechanically adapted via test-only helpers. The previous direct invalid-surface Render test is now a Configured-Agent invalid-vendor rejection check, because callers no longer pass arbitrary provenance into Render. Existing success/error, replay, tool, persistence and lifecycle assertions are retained.
+
+`chapter6_test.go` covers all-provider canonical parity, signatures/replay filtering, response-local zero IDs, delayed local progress before completion, clean history, reader/framing failures, stream opt-outs, arrival-order tool authorization, cancellation/recovery, trace redaction, callback ownership and an actual gated CLI subprocess. `cmd/ch01/display_test.go` stalls the display consumer and checks fast admission and ordered complete drain. The inherited suite continues to cover hint/job/batch-budget/restore/HTTP errors and genuine long-lived requests.
+
+`evidence/ch06/attempts.md` retains mistakes and assistance. `evidence/ch06/controls/receipts.json` binds exact mutations, successful compile, expected behavioral failure, byte-identical restoration and restored pass for live-delta suppression, dropped ID zero and final-order-only tool authorization. These are small focused controls, not an alternate runner or implementation.
+
+No prompt-content trigger, fake production response path, fixture-specific configuration exception, second dispatcher, or future capability framework was added.
